    "body": "- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\nAutomatically change directory to a project root.\r\nMain inspiration is a \"mattn/vim-findroot\" and \"airblade/vim-rooter\" plugin.\r\n\r\nFeatures:\r\n- Automatically change directory to a project root defined in\r\n `MiniAutochdir.config`. The patterns are written in the glob pattern.\r\n\r\nGeneral overview of how to find a project root:\r\n- Check whether the designated file or the directory exists in current directory.\r\n- If not, move to the parent directory to find a project root.",
    "closed_at": "2022-06-14T12:43:11Z",
    "comment_data": [],
    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "I am really, **really** grateful for all your time and effort you put in this PR. But please, before committing yourself to doing some code related work, make sure that it aligns with project vision. To avoid situations like this, I explicitly put [those lines](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/066688ae4aa3c7293009cc62768495a149e9ef9c/CONTRIBUTING.md?plain=1#L11) in contributing guidelines (maybe should make them more visible, to be honest).\r\n\r\nThe 'airblade/vim-rooter' is an essential part of my workflow. For the long time I had certain ideas of what would make its good replacement in 'mini.nvim' (probably, named 'mini.root'). So I came up with a more \"functional\" design where user can define a root-finding function (instead of patterns) as a predicate for a directory path. It will work by traversing ancestors of buffer path and stopping when predicate gives `true`. To make it more convenient, there would be some predicate helpers (like `has_child()`, `has_sibling()`, etc).\r\n\r\nSo unfortunately, your implementation doesn't go well with this intended design. I am not opposed to someone else making this module, but there will be **a lot of small details** that I want to get right with it. If you are willing to spend considerably more time on it, I am happy to collaborate.",
            "created_at": "2022-06-14T07:44:01Z",
